    "key_shape": "{'static': {literal: {kind: count}}, 'dynamic': {expression: {kind: count}}}",
    "why": "DF-15. The Section 8.4 no-loss gate counts a citation only if its literal resolves onto an article in that edition articles_8_4.json, and _scan_citations drops a non-8.4 literal at SCAN time before that, so Part 4/5/6 citations could be deleted without failing anything. Measured 2026-09-18 at SITE level: the 8.4 gate covers 105 distinct source sites and this baseline covers 178 - about 62 percent of the citation surface was ungated. (The 8.4 baseline total of 213 is a PER-EDITION count, 96 for 2020 plus 117 for 2025, of those same 105 sites; comparing it against a per-site figure understates the gap, and an earlier draft of this file did exactly that.) Not all of these are Part 4/5/6: about 20 dynamic sites are variables bound to 8.4 f-strings and three are the data-driven coverage emitter in btap/audit, so the honest description is \"sites the 8.4 scanner cannot count\". This baseline does NOT widen the coverage attestation, which is a Section 8.4 document and stays one.",
    "two_populations": "static: a plain string literal, keyed exactly as {literal: {kind: count}} with no file path in the key so a rename cannot invalidate it. A literal is included when it carries no 8.4 token at all, and ALSO when an article-shaped reference survives removing its 8.4 tokens - a mixed citation such as 8.4.4.12.; 5.2.2.7.(1) is counted by the 8.4 gate under 8.4.4.12, so deleting its Part 5 half used to fire nothing anywhere. dynamic: an article= that is a variable, a subscript or an f-string the scanner cannot fold to a name, keyed by ast.unparse of the expression - the code own text, not the scanner truncated rendering of it. Keyed rather than merely counted because a bare total is blind to a swap.",
    "complement_is_derived": "The set of gated sites comes from CoverageGenerator.citations_for itself, not from a copy of its scan-time regex, so no hole can open between the two gates and there is no duplicated predicate to drift."
  }
